<LessonPage page={page}>
<main class="wrap" data-core-duration="60–70 minutes" data-extension-duration="15–20 minutes">
  <p class="kicker"><span class="dot"></span> Grammar · Lesson 11</p>
  <h1>Future review: <em>choose the present viewpoint on a later event</em></h1>
  <p class="standfirst">English does not have one neutral “future tense” that replaces every other choice. <span class="ex">I’ll call</span>, <span class="ex">I’m going to call</span>, and <span class="ex">I’m calling at three</span> can point toward a later call, but each form presents the speaker’s current relationship to it: a response or prediction, a prior intention or present trajectory, or an arrangement treated as already organized.</p>
  <div class="meta-row">
    <span class="tag">60–70 min core</span>
    <span class="tag">will · be going to · present continuous</span>
    <span class="tag">response · intention · arrangement</span>
    <span class="tag">15–20 min listening and retrieval</span>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson-flow" aria-label="Lesson flow">
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Notice</b><span>Recover when and how each plan already exists.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Contrast</b><span>Separate viewpoint tendencies from rigid signal-word rules.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Connect</b><span>Add schedules and future time-clause boundaries.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Use</b><span>Coordinate a fictional community technology fair.</span></div>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line"><b>Can-do:</b> By the end, you can use <span class="ex">will</span> for immediate responses, offers, promises, willingness, and neutral predictions; use <span class="ex">be going to</span> for prior intentions and forecasts that foreground a present trajectory; use the Present Continuous for arrangements supported by practical details; recognize the Present Simple for official schedules; keep future time and condition clauses in the Present Simple; explain genuine areas of overlap; and make contractions and informal <span class="ex">going to</span> reductions recoverable in connected U.S. speech.</p>

  <h2>Two-minute retrieval</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You recovered an immediate will response, a going-to intention that existed before speaking, and a present-continuous arrangement with a confirmed time.">
    <h3>Reactivate three future viewpoints</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Future review foundation choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="I’ll carry it">I’ll carry it</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="are going to rent">are going to rent</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="are meeting">are meeting</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="carry it yesterday">carry it yesterday</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The box breaks during setup. Mika responds at that moment: “<button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="I’ll carry it" data-hint="Use will for the helpful response made now." data-fix="I’ll carry it to the storage room.">choose</button> to the storage room.”</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The team decided yesterday and recorded the intention: They <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="are going to rent" data-hint="The decision existed before this conversation, so present it as a prior intention." data-fix="They are going to rent a backup projector.">choose</button> a backup projector.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The vendor accepted a 3:00 appointment for tomorrow: We <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="are meeting" data-hint="A confirmed appointment with a specific time supports the present-continuous arrangement viewpoint." data-fix="We are meeting the vendor at three tomorrow.">choose</button> the vendor at three tomorrow.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Notice one event plan from four present viewpoints</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Harborline technology fair, Wednesday at 4:00 p.m.</h3>
    <p><b>New problem:</b> The main projector has just failed. <span class="ex">Mika: “I’ll call the repair desk now.”</span></p>
    <p><b>Yesterday’s decision:</b> The team approved a backup rental. <span class="ex">Ravi: “We’re going to rent a backup projector.”</span></p>
    <p><b>Confirmed appointment:</b> The vendor accepted 3:00 p.m. Thursday. <span class="ex">Mika: “We’re meeting the vendor at three tomorrow.”</span></p>
    <p><b>Official timetable:</b> The demonstration begins at 10:00 a.m. Friday. <span class="ex">Ravi: “The demonstration starts at ten on Friday.”</span></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Which action is a new response?</li>
      <li>Which intention existed before this conversation?</li>
      <li>Which plan is supported by an appointment, and which event belongs to a published schedule?</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You linked will to a new response, going to to the prior decision, the present continuous to a confirmed appointment, and the Present Simple to the official timetable.">
    <h3>Discovery check: recover the source of each future</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Future viewpoint discovery choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="a response decided now">a response decided now</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="a prior team intention">a prior team intention</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="a confirmed arrangement">a confirmed arrangement</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="an official schedule">an official schedule</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span><span class="ex">I’ll call the repair desk now</span> presents <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="a response decided now" data-hint="The projector failure has just created the need, and Mika responds immediately." data-fix="The call is a response decided now.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span><span class="ex">We’re going to rent a backup</span> presents <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="a prior team intention" data-hint="The approval happened yesterday, before the current conversation." data-fix="The rental is a prior team intention.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span><span class="ex">We’re meeting the vendor at three tomorrow</span> presents <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="a confirmed arrangement" data-hint="The vendor accepted a specific appointment." data-fix="The vendor meeting is a confirmed arrangement.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span><span class="ex">The demonstration starts at ten on Friday</span> presents <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="an official schedule" data-hint="The time belongs to the fair’s published timetable." data-fix="The 10:00 demonstration is an official schedule item.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Get to the root: all three forms express a present stance</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>The event is later, but the grammar shows how the speaker organizes it <b>now</b>. A form is not a label attached permanently to one real-world event. After Mika says <span class="ex">I’ll call</span>, she may later report the same call as an intention: <span class="ex">I’m going to call at five</span>. If the repair desk gives her an appointment, she may say <span class="ex">I’m speaking with the technician at five</span>.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>viewpoint</th><th>complete form</th><th>one clear example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>response, willingness, promise, or prediction</td><td><span class="ex">will / won’t + base verb</span></td><td><span class="ex">I’ll check it. I think it will work.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>prior intention or present trajectory</td><td><span class="ex">am / is / are + going to + base verb</span></td><td><span class="ex">We’re going to rent one.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>arrangement treated as organized</td><td><span class="ex">am / is / are + verb-ing + future context</span></td><td><span class="ex">We’re meeting at three tomorrow.</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Negatives and questions keep each system intact: <span class="ex">I won’t call. Will you call?</span> <span class="ex">We aren’t going to cancel. Are they going to rent one?</span> <span class="ex">She isn’t meeting us. Are you meeting the vendor?</span> Do not combine systems as <span class="ex">will going to</span>, and do not remove <span class="ex">be</span> from either <span class="ex">going to</span> or the Present Continuous.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You kept will with a base verb, preserved be in going-to and present-continuous forms, and built their negative and question patterns without mixing systems.">
    <h3>Form check: keep one complete system</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Future form choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="won’t cancel">won’t cancel</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Are they going to rent">Are they going to rent</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="is meeting">is meeting</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Will you test">Will you test</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="aren’t going to move">aren’t going to move</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The team has promised to continue: We <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="won’t cancel" data-hint="Negative will is won’t followed by base cancel." data-fix="We won’t cancel the fair.">choose</button> the fair.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Ask about the team’s prior intention: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Are they going to rent" data-hint="Move are before the subject, then keep going to + base rent." data-fix="Are they going to rent a backup projector?">choose</button> a backup projector?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The vendor appointment is confirmed: Mika <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="is meeting" data-hint="Present Continuous requires is before meeting for singular Mika." data-fix="Mika is meeting the vendor at three.">choose</button> the vendor at three.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>Ask Ravi to volunteer for the technical check: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Will you test" data-hint="Use Will you + base verb for the requested action." data-fix="Will you test the video link?">choose</button> the video link?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The original room remains the team’s prior plan: They <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="aren’t going to move" data-hint="Negative going-to keeps are, adds not, then uses going to + base move." data-fix="They aren’t going to move the demonstration.">choose</button> the demonstration.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Use <span class="ex">will</span> for a response, commitment, willingness, or neutral prediction</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p><span class="ex">Will</span> commonly presents a choice made as part of the current exchange: <span class="ex">The cable is missing. I’ll get one.</span> It also makes offers and commitments, expresses willingness or refusal, and gives a prediction without presenting it specifically as the outcome of a current visible trajectory.</p>
  <p><span class="ex">Will you ...?</span> can ask about a future event, willingness, or a requested action. Context and tone decide the function: <span class="ex">Will the vendor arrive at three?</span> asks for a prediction or fact; <span class="ex">Will you carry this box?</span> normally asks the listener to act. The phrase <span class="ex">I think</span> often accompanies <span class="ex">will</span>, but it is not a mechanical signal word.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You used will for an immediate response, an offer, a promise, a willing refusal to expose a password, and a neutral attendance prediction.">
    <h3>Will check: identify the conversational job</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Will function choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="I’ll replace it">I’ll replace it</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="I’ll carry them">I’ll carry them</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="I’ll send it">I’ll send it</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="won’t share">won’t share</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="will attract">will attract</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>A cable breaks while Mika is speaking. She decides at that moment: “<button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="I’ll replace it" data-hint="The decision is a direct response to the newly broken cable." data-fix="I’ll replace it now.">choose</button> now.”</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Ravi sees that Mika’s boxes are heavy and volunteers: “<button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="I’ll carry them" data-hint="Use will for the offer made in response to the visible need." data-fix="I’ll carry them for you.">choose</button> for you.”</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Mika makes a firm commitment before the 5:00 deadline: “<button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="I’ll send it" data-hint="Use will for the promise made to the listener." data-fix="I’ll send it before five.">choose</button> before five.”</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>Ravi refuses a dangerous request: “I <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="won’t share" data-hint="Won’t can express refusal or unwillingness, not only a predicted negative event." data-fix="I won’t share the event password in public.">choose</button> the event password in public.”</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The coordinator gives a neutral estimate from the registration trend: “I think the fair <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="will attract" data-hint="The speaker presents a forecast, not a prior intention or arrangement." data-fix="I think the fair will attract about two hundred visitors.">choose</button> about two hundred visitors.”</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Use <span class="ex">be going to</span> for a prior intention or present trajectory</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>An intention already exists before the current words: <span class="ex">We decided yesterday. We’re going to rent a projector.</span> For a prediction, <span class="ex">going to</span> can foreground what the speaker sees as a present cause, trajectory, or sign: <span class="ex">The battery is at one percent. It’s going to shut down.</span></p>
  <p>The evidence does not make the predicted outcome logically certain, and the form does not guarantee that an intention will happen. Nor does present evidence make <span class="ex">will</span> ungrammatical in every surrounding context. The controlled items state which viewpoint the speaker intends to foreground.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You presented a previously approved intention, a canceled prior intention, an intention question, and two forecasts that foreground clear present trajectories.">
    <h3>Going-to check: recover the prior basis or current trajectory</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Going to function choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="are going to rent">are going to rent</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="isn’t going to print">isn’t going to print</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Are you going to invite">Are you going to invite</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="is going to shut down">is going to shut down</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="is going to fall">is going to fall</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The signed Tuesday decision is still active: We <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="are going to rent" data-hint="The intention was approved before the current report." data-fix="We are going to rent a backup projector.">choose</button> a backup projector.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Ravi had planned paper badges, but the team canceled that intention this morning: He <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="isn’t going to print" data-hint="Use negative be going to for the prior intention that is no longer active." data-fix="He isn’t going to print paper badges.">choose</button> paper badges.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Ask whether Mika already intends to invite the repair technician: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Are you going to invite" data-hint="Use Are you going to + base verb to ask about an existing intention." data-fix="Are you going to invite the repair technician?">choose</button> the repair technician?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The tablet shows one percent and no charger is connected. Foreground that current trajectory: It <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="is going to shut down" data-hint="The current battery state points toward the forecast." data-fix="It is going to shut down.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The display stand is leaning farther every second. Foreground the visible trajectory: It <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="is going to fall" data-hint="The present movement is the basis the speaker chooses to foreground." data-fix="It is going to fall.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Use the Present Continuous for an arrangement, then recognize a schedule</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>A future Present Continuous presents a plan as organized through a time, place, booking, ticket, participant, or comparable practical commitment: <span class="ex">We’re meeting the vendor at three.</span> It need not always involve a second person: <span class="ex">I’m flying to Denver on Friday</span> can be an arrangement because the ticket exists. A future-time expression or surrounding context keeps the form from being read as an action in progress now.</p>
  <p>Official timetables and programs commonly use the Present Simple: <span class="ex">The train leaves at 8:10. The fair opens at nine.</span> This is a useful boundary, not a claim that speakers can never choose another form. A person may say <span class="ex">I’m taking the 8:10 train</span> to present their own booked arrangement.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You used the Present Continuous for appointments and booked travel, then the Present Simple for the train and fair’s official schedules.">
    <h3>Arrangement or schedule check</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Arrangement and schedule choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="are meeting">are meeting</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="is seeing">is seeing</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="am flying">am flying</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="leaves">leaves</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="opens">opens</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The vendor accepted the calendar invitation for Thursday at three: We <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="are meeting" data-hint="The accepted invitation and specific time support an arrangement viewpoint." data-fix="We are meeting the vendor at three on Thursday.">choose</button> the vendor at three on Thursday.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Mika has a confirmed technician appointment at 2:15: She <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="is seeing" data-hint="The confirmed appointment supports the Present Continuous." data-fix="She is seeing the technician at 2:15.">choose</button> the technician at 2:15.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Ravi bought his Friday ticket and received a seat number: “I <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="am flying" data-hint="A ticket and seat number can organize one person’s travel as an arrangement." data-fix="I am flying to Denver on Friday.">choose</button> to Denver on Friday.”</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>According to the railway’s official timetable, the first train <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="leaves" data-hint="Use the Present Simple to foreground the published railway schedule." data-fix="The first train leaves at 8:10.">choose</button> at 8:10.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>According to the fair’s printed program, the main hall <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="opens" data-hint="Use the Present Simple to foreground the official program time." data-fix="The main hall opens at nine.">choose</button> at nine.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Inside a future time or condition clause, use the Present Simple</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>In the main clause, <span class="ex">will</span> can express the later result. After common future connectors such as <span class="ex">when, after, before, as soon as, until</span>, and conditional <span class="ex">if</span>, English normally uses a present form rather than <span class="ex">will</span>: <span class="ex">I’ll call when I arrive</span>, not <span class="ex">when I will arrive</span>. The present form still points to future time because the connector supplies that relationship.</p>
  <p>This section targets ordinary future time and open-condition clauses. It does not claim that <span class="ex">will</span> can never occur after these words in every possible meaning or advanced discourse use.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You kept will in the result clause and used the Present Simple after when, as soon as, if, and until to mark ordinary future time or condition relationships.">
    <h3>Future-clause check: do not duplicate <span class="ex">will</span></h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Future time clause choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="arrive">arrive</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="finishes">finishes</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="rains">rains</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="confirms">confirms</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>I’ll call the vendor when I <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="arrive" data-hint="After future-time when, use the Present Simple arrive." data-fix="I’ll call the vendor when I arrive.">choose</button> at the venue.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>As soon as Ravi <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="finishes" data-hint="After as soon as, use third-person singular Present Simple finishes." data-fix="As soon as Ravi finishes the test, he’ll send the result.">choose</button> the test, he’ll send the result.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>If it <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="rains" data-hint="In this open future condition, use the Present Simple after if." data-fix="If it rains, we’ll move the outdoor table inside.">choose</button>, we’ll move the outdoor table inside.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>We won’t publish the room number until the venue <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="confirms" data-hint="After future-time until, use Present Simple confirms." data-fix="We won’t publish the room number until the venue confirms it.">choose</button> it.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Choose a viewpoint, not a signal word</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>Real speakers sometimes have more than one grammatical choice. <span class="ex">It will rain</span> can present a neutral forecast; <span class="ex">It’s going to rain</span> can foreground a present trajectory. <span class="ex">We’re going to meet the vendor</span> can present an intention; <span class="ex">We’re meeting the vendor at three</span> can present the same plan as an arrangement. The point is not to discover which form the event “really is.” The point is to choose what to foreground now.</p>
  <p>Nearby words do not decide alone. <span class="ex">I think it will rain</span> is common, and <span class="ex">I think it’s going to rain</span> is also natural when the speaker foregrounds present evidence. Likewise, a future Present Continuous needs future context; <span class="ex">Mika is meeting the vendor</span> could mean now unless the time or situation locates it later.</p>
  <div class="quiz" data-quiz data-success-message="Correct. You distinguished neutral forecast from present trajectory, intention from arrangement, a signal-word tendency from a rule, and present from future readings of the Present Continuous.">
    <h3>Viewpoint check: explain what the speaker foregrounds</h3>
    <div class="quiz-list">
