  <h2>Sound natural: keep the form recoverable through reduction</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>In connected U.S. speech, <span class="ex">I’ll</span> is often /aɪl/, <span class="ex">we’ll</span> /wil/, and <span class="ex">won’t</span> /woʊnt/. <span class="ex">Going to</span> may be careful /ˈɡoʊɪŋ tə/ or reduce informally toward /ˈɡənə/. The reduced pronunciation still needs a form of <span class="ex">be</span> and a following base verb: <span class="ex">We’re gonna rent one</span>, not <span class="ex">We gonna</span>. Use the full spelling <span class="ex">going to</span> in standard edited writing.</p>
  <p>Present-continuous arrangements normally contract <span class="ex">I am, we are</span>, and <span class="ex">she is</span> in speech: <span class="ex">I’m flying Friday. We’re meeting at three. She’s seeing the technician.</span> Sentence stress highlights the contrast the conversation needs: <span class="ex">We’re meeting on THURSday, not FRIday</span>. No grammar form permanently owns the strongest stress.</p>
